WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.418
Batch size tuning is bounded by hardware.

00:00:02.418 --> 00:00:07.054
GPU VRAM is usually the first constraint people notice because model weights, runtime buffers,

00:00:07.054 --> 00:00:09.703
and the KV cache all compete for space.

00:00:09.703 --> 00:00:13.345
System RAM matters too, especially when the model is partially offloaded,

00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:15.994
when the runtime uses CPU memory for staging,

00:00:15.994 --> 00:00:18.643
or when the machine falls back to swapping.

00:00:18.643 --> 00:00:23.279
Once swapping begins, performance can collapse even if the process does not crash immediately.

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:27.253
Memory bandwidth and compute utilization are just as important as raw capacity.

00:00:27.253 --> 00:00:32.551
A GPU with enough VRAM may still be underused if the runtime cannot feed it efficiently.

00:00:32.551 --> 00:00:37.187
A CPU-only setup may be limited by memory bandwidth, thread scheduling, and cache behavior.

00:00:37.187 --> 00:00:39.505
Integrated GPUs share memory with the system,

00:00:39.505 --> 00:00:43.810
so aggressive settings can affect both inference and the rest of the machine.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:49.108
Context length has a direct impact because longer conversations and larger documents require more attention state.

00:00:49.108 --> 00:00:53.081
The KV cache stores intermediate state that lets generation continue without reprocessing

00:00:53.081 --> 00:00:54.737
the entire context every time.

00:00:54.737 --> 00:00:57.717
That cache is useful, but it is not free.

00:00:57.717 --> 00:00:59.373
More concurrent sequences, longer contexts,

00:00:59.373 --> 00:01:02.353
and larger batch-related settings can increase memory pressure quickly.

00:01:02.353 --> 00:01:05.995
Oversized batch settings can look attractive because they promise more parallelism,

00:01:05.995 --> 00:01:09.638
but they can also cause out-of-memory errors, runtime instability, high latency,

00:01:09.638 --> 00:01:10.631
or slower throughput.

00:01:10.631 --> 00:01:14.605
The practical goal is to find a setting that keeps the hardware

00:01:14.605 --> 00:01:17.254
busy without pushing it into a fragile state.

00:01:17.254 --> 00:01:18.247
Stable headroom matters,

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:22.883
especially for systems that need to run for hours instead of surviving one benchmark.

00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:26.194
This is why a stable configuration usually leaves deliberate headroom.

00:01:26.194 --> 00:01:30.168
A machine that reports just enough free VRAM during a clean test

00:01:30.168 --> 00:01:32.486
may fail when the prompt is longer,

00:01:32.486 --> 00:01:34.804
another process opens, or concurrent requests arrive.

00:01:34.804 --> 00:01:36.460
Headroom is not wasted capacity.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:40.433
It is the margin that keeps inference predictable when real operating conditions

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.420
are less tidy than a benchmark.
